CHANGELOG: two and a half months of history, and a trigger that will keep it

The CHANGELOG stopped at alpha.5 on 2026-06-03. Thirty-seven git-records and
dev-journal chapters 41 through 73 accumulated behind it — which is to say the single
largest and most consequential stretch of the project's history was invisible to anyone
who was not reading commits.

THE BLOCK. `[Unreleased]` now covers that span, organized for a reader outside the tree
rather than as thirty-seven bullet points. The through-line: DRAKE arrived complete —
specification, emitter, parser, CLI, and two round-trip gates over 91 definitions in two
corpora; element values became normative expression trees, which is what lets a target
other than Scala read a rule condition at all; loading left the Generator for TypeLoader
and an explicit DefinitionPath that resolves unique-or-error over held roots; the
framework began validating its own foundation through the rule engine it ships, driven by
its own domain acting as an actor; and YAML was retired as a definition language.

Also recorded: CodecAspect as a fifth aspect, Local as an eleventh element kind, the
format domain with its JSON and XML sub-domains, aspect composition, Assembly as actor
groups in data, the capability domains, the media example chain and its first
meaning-preserving cross-domain transform, structural TypeName identity, and Map as the
first type constructor to leave the host's vocabulary for `{K, V}`.

On the Changed side the four that alter how definitions are written: uniform root meaning
(absent derivation MEANS derives-DracoType, and 27 explicit ones were stripped with
byte-identical output), role is presence rather than name (the .rule/.actor suffixes gone
from filenames and from projected names), RuleAspect restructured so variables and
conditions live inside the pattern and a condition's parameters are derived rather than
stored, and actor-ness resting on the aspect alone.

THE TRIGGER, which is the part meant to outlast the block. A CHANGELOG entry is now
written at the same moment as the commit's git-record, in the same commit. That is stated
in CHANGELOG.md itself and in DRACO.md's operating rules, and the division is explicit:
the record carries the reasoning, the CHANGELOG carries one or two sentences of
observable fact.

The choice of trigger is not arbitrary and it is the same argument that produced the
DRACO.md rewrite two days ago. A record file already has to be written before every
commit, and that step has held all week without anyone thinking about it. Release time
does not work — it is exactly what was being relied on, and this file went 75 days and 37
commits without one. Attaching the entry to a step that reliably happens costs nothing;
attaching it to a boundary that may never arrive costs everything, and did.

Two artifacts in this repo already demonstrate both outcomes. Auto-memory is updated as
part of doing the work and is accurate. This file waited for a release and fell a quarter
of a year behind.
